<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-ble">
    <title>Bluetooth Low Energy</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <term>Bluetooth Low Energy</term> (BLE) is a short-range wireless protocol designed
            for infrequent, low-volume data exchange with minimal power consumption.  Rather than
            implementing the BLE stack on the STM32 directly, this chapter uses the Adafruit
            Bluefruit LE UART Friend, a self-contained BLE module that communicates with the
            STM32 over a familiar UART interface.  The module handles all BLE protocol details,
            leaving the firmware free to read and write ASCII strings.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Describe the BLE GAP and GATT layers and their roles.</li>
                <li>Explain the difference between BLE central and peripheral roles.</li>
                <li>Configure USART1 on the STM32 to communicate with the Bluefruit module.</li>
                <li>Use the Bluefruit Connect app to exchange data with the STM32 wirelessly.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================
         CANVAS QUIZ QUESTIONS (Before-Class)
         Q1. A fitness tracker broadcasts its presence so your phone can find it.
             Which BLE role is the fitness tracker playing?
             A) Central — it initiates the connection
             B) Peripheral — it advertises and waits to be connected to (CORRECT)
             C) Server — it hosts the GATT database
             D) Scanner — it searches for other devices
             Explanation: The peripheral advertises; the central (phone) scans
             and initiates. The fitness tracker is a peripheral.

         Q2. After a BLE connection is established, what stops?
             A) Data exchange between the two devices
             B) The GATT server on the peripheral
             C) Advertising — the peripheral no longer needs to announce itself (CORRECT)
             D) The phone's ability to read characteristics
             Explanation: Advertising is only needed during discovery. Once the
             central has connected, the peripheral stops broadcasting and both
             sides communicate using GATT.

         Q3. In GATT, what is a "characteristic"?
             A) The name the peripheral broadcasts during advertising
             B) A typed, addressable data value that the central can read, write,
                or subscribe to (CORRECT)
             C) The encryption key used for the BLE connection
             D) The physical radio channel the connection uses
             Explanation: A GATT characteristic is the smallest unit of data —
             a value with a type (UUID), permissions (read/write/notify), and
             a current value. For example, the Bluefruit's UART service has
             TX and RX characteristics.

         Q4. Why do we use the Adafruit Bluefruit module instead of implementing
             BLE directly on the STM32?
             A) The STM32C031C6 does not have enough GPIO pins for BLE
             B) BLE requires a 5 V supply that the STM32 cannot provide
             C) A complete BLE protocol stack is extremely complex to implement;
